<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Role in the World</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How the Canal Impacts Panama's Future</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Paraphrase articles and texts for clearer understanding. Communication: Aid in negotiating group roles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can paraphrase articles on the Panama Canal's impact on international trade, using visuals like a map comparing shipping routes through the Canal vs. around Cape Horn (e.g., "The map shows that using the Canal saves two weeks of travel time for ships.")
            Can help negotiate roles in group projects on global trade, using gestures and encouraging eye contact to keep focus (e.g., "Would you like to focus on the economic or environmental aspects of the Canal?")
            Can clarify main ideas in trade-related texts for group work, using visuals like pie charts to illustrate key concepts (e.g., "Our project will cover how the Canal influences trade and local jobs.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a world map showing shipping routes and asks students to recall the key advantages of the Panama Canal. "What significant impact has the Panama Canal had on global shipping routes?" (Expected: "It reduces travel time and costs."). Teacher then introduces the idea of 'mediation' as clarifying or simplifying complex information, or helping people understand each other.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a simple, complex sentence about trade, e.g., "The comprehensive operational guidelines, meticulously formulated by the Panama Canal Authority, have significantly enhanced the efficiency and safety of maritime transit through the waterway." The teacher then models paraphrasing this sentence simply: "The Canal's operations are carefully managed by its authority to improve shipping efficiency and safety." The teacher emphasizes that the core idea is preserved. Pronunciation of /oʊ/ in 'global' and 'ocean' is modeled, along with the stress pattern of 'environmental' and 'impactful'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I paraphrase, do I just copy the sentence, or do I explain it in simpler words?" (Expected: "Explain in simpler words.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a group member doesn't understand a complex trade term, what can I do to mediate their understanding?" (Expected: "Explain it to them, perhaps using an example or a visual.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Panama's Role in the World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, factual news article excerpt (approx. 150 words) about a recent challenge or investment related to the Panama Canal, e.g., "Panama Canal Faces Climate Change Impact on Water Levels" or "New Investments Enhance Canal's Future Capacity." The text will naturally contain vocabulary like 'operation', 'traffic', 'investments', 'environmental groups', 'impact', 'sustainability'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discussing the canal's impact (e.g., "How has the Panama Canal been impacting global trade over the past few decades?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the article. Teacher asks gist questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main challenge or opportunity discussed in the article regarding the Panama Canal's future?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"According to the text, which groups or entities are involved in addressing this issue?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If these issues had not been addressed, what might have been the consequences for global shipping?" (Integrating third conditional here)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice paraphrasing complex sentences using passive voice, second/third conditionals, and Present Perfect Continuous, incorporating vocabulary such as 'efficiency', 'investments', 'environmental concerns', 'influence', 'impact'. Pragmatic skill: expressing hypothetical ideas and discussing impact respectfully.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Paraphrasing (Cloze & Match):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 complex sentences related to the Canal (e.g., "The continued modernization of its infrastructure has been significantly improving the Canal's operational efficiency."). Students are given a simpler, paraphrased version with blanks (e.g., "The Canal's _______ (infrastructure/routes) has been _______ (improved/monitored), leading to better _______ (efficiency/delays)."). Students fill in the blanks using a word bank of target vocabulary and then match the complex sentence to its simplified version.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Conditional Transformations:</b> Students are given a scenario (e.g., "The Panama Canal faced extreme drought last year.") and prompted to rephrase it using a conditional, then paraphrase the outcome. "If the Canal had not adjusted its traffic management, significant delays would have been caused for global shipping." (Third conditional - passive voice). They then rephrase a prediction: "The government is considering major investments for water sustainability." (e.g., "If new investments are made, the Canal's future operations will be more sustainable.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Mediation Drill:</b> In pairs, students take turns reading a complex sentence (provided on cards) about global trade or the Canal (e.g., "Many environmental groups have been raising concerns about the potential ecological impact of the expanded Canal.") to their partner. The partner must then paraphrase it, focusing on clarifying the main idea and using at least one target vocabulary word (e.g., "So, environmental groups are worried about the Canal's negative *impact* on ecosystems."). They provide peer feedback on clarity and accurate use of vocabulary and grammar, practicing respectful academic language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Panama's Role in the World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Global Trade Negotiation Simulation Segment</b><br>
                Students continue their "Global Trade Simulation" project. In groups, they assume roles (e.g., a Panamanian government official, a shipping company representative, an environmental advocate). Each group is presented with a hypothetical scenario related to the Canal's future (e.g., "A proposal to increase transit fees to fund environmental protection initiatives" or "A new technology promises to reduce shipping delays but requires significant infrastructure investments.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Each role receives a brief, slightly complex position statement (e.g., "Our global shipping firm's profitability has been continuously monitored, and any unilateral adjustments to Canal costs would severely impact our operational budget.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students within their groups must mediate their character's position to the others. They must paraphrase their statement for clarity and negotiate potential solutions. They should use second conditionals to predict outcomes ("If transit fees were significantly raised, shipping traffic would likely be diverted to other routes, affecting Panama's revenue."), and passive voice to describe actions ("The new policy could be implemented by the authority next year.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Students practice clarifying main ideas, asking for clarification, and using gestures/eye contact to manage group focus during negotiations. They must respectfully discuss the Canal's long-term impact on global trade and local ecosystems.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Each group presents their negotiated solution, explaining the compromises made and how different perspectives were mediated.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the Global Trade Negotiation Simulation (Stage 4). Students are assessed on their ability to paraphrase complex information, negotiate group roles effectively, and clarify main ideas during the group discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Can paraphrase complex ideas into simpler terms effectively, making the main points clear to group members. (Uses phrases like "In simpler terms...", "What I mean is...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Language:</b> Appropriately uses passive voice, second/third conditionals (e.g., "If a new fee were imposed, global shipping would face higher costs."), Present Perfect Continuous, and target vocabulary (e.g., 'influence', 'investments', 'sustainability').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Negotiation Skills:</b> Aids in negotiating group roles and perspectives using appropriate pragmatic and sociolinguistic language (e.g., "Would you prefer to lead the discussion on economic impact or environmental concerns?") and non-verbal cues (gestures, eye contact).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of key vocabulary, including the long /oʊ/ sound and appropriate stress in multisyllabic words like 'environmental'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively listens to others, encourages participation, and helps resolve misunderstandings.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short reflection:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one piece of complex information you successfully mediated for your group today, and how did you do it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using conditionals help you express hypothetical situations and predict outcomes during the negotiation?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn about mediating complex information or group discussions today that I can use in future projects or real-life situations?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has my understanding of the Panama Canal's global impact and future challenges grown throughout this unit?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The negotiation simulation effectively engaged students in applying mediation skills in a realistic context. The integration of conditionals for hypothetical situations was particularly strong. Some students still struggle with distinguishing between second and third conditionals and consistently applying the passive voice in spontaneous speech. More explicit mini-lessons or practice on these specific grammar points, perhaps through targeted sentence building exercises, would be beneficial. The importance of non-verbal cues in mediation could be further emphasized. Overall, the project served as an excellent culmination for the unit.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "How the Canal Impacts Panama's Future" theme. The skills developed, especially mediation and critical thinking about global issues, will be transferable to future themes focusing on Panama's broader role in international relations and sustainable development.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>